   "cite": "eq-uc-particular",
   "prompt": "<p>Check that $\\ky$ really solves the equation <a href=\"#eq-2-6\">(2.6)</a> and the given initial conditions.</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "solution": "Differentiate $\\ky = \\frac{3 e^{-2x} - 2 e^{-3x} + 3x-1}{9}$: $\\kyprime = \\frac{-6e^{-2x} + 6e^{-3x} + 3}{9}$ and $\\kydprime = \\frac{12e^{-2x} - 18e^{-3x}}{9}$. Then $\\kydprime + 5\\kyprime + 6\\ky = \\frac{(12 - 30 + 18)\\,e^{-2x} + (-18 + 30 - 12)\\,e^{-3x} + 15 + 18x - 6}{9} = \\frac{18x + 9}{9} = 2x + 1$, which is (2.6). At $x = 0$: $\\ky(0) = \\frac{3 - 2 - 1}{9} = 0$ and $\\kyprime(0) = \\frac{-6 + 6 + 3}{9} = \\frac{1}{3}$, the given initial conditions."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "e2",
   "source_id": "2.5.2",
   "source_number": "2.5.2",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Apply",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "uc-guess",
   "prompt": "<p>Find a particular solution of $\\kydprime-\\kyprime -6\\ky = e^{2x}$.</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "solution": "$\\kyp = -\\frac{1}{4}e^{2x}$. The characteristic equation $r^2 - r - 6 = (r-3)(r+2) = 0$ has roots $3$ and $-2$, so $\\kyc = C_1 e^{3x} + C_2 e^{-2x}$ and $e^{2x}$ does not duplicate it. Try $\\kyp = Ae^{2x}$: $\\kypprime = 2Ae^{2x}$, $\\kypdprime = 4Ae^{2x}$, and $\\kypdprime - \\kypprime - 6\\kyp = (4 - 2 - 6)\\,Ae^{2x} = -4Ae^{2x}$. So $-4A = 1$ and $A = -\\frac{1}{4}$."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "e3",
   "source_id": "2.5.3",
   "source_number": "2.5.3",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Apply",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "duplication-twice",
   "prompt": "<p>Find a particular solution of $\\kydprime-4\\kyprime +4\\ky = e^{2x}$.</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "solution": "$\\kyp = \\frac{1}{2}x^2 e^{2x}$. The characteristic equation $r^2 - 4r + 4 = (r-2)^2 = 0$ has the double root $2$, so $\\kyc = C_1 e^{2x} + C_2 x e^{2x}$, and both $Ae^{2x}$ and $Axe^{2x}$ duplicate it. Multiply by $x$ until the duplication is gone: $\\kyp = Ax^2 e^{2x}$. Then $\\kypprime = (2Ax + 2Ax^2)\\,e^{2x}$ and $\\kypdprime = (2A + 8Ax + 4Ax^2)\\,e^{2x}$, so $\\kypdprime - 4\\kypprime + 4\\kyp = (2A + 8Ax + 4Ax^2 - 8Ax - 8Ax^2 + 4Ax^2)\\,e^{2x} = 2Ae^{2x}$. So $2A = 1$ and $A = \\frac{1}{2}$."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "e4",
   "source_id": "2.5.4",
   "source_number": "2.5.4",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Apply",
   "place": {
