   },
   "cite": "duplication-trick",
   "prompt": "<p>Solve the initial value problem $\\kydprime+9\\ky = \\cos (3x) + \\sin (3x)$ for $\\ky(0) = 2$, $\\kyprime(0) = 1$.</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "solution": "$\\ky = 2\\cos(3x) + \\frac{7}{18}\\sin(3x) + \\frac{x}{6}\\bigl(\\sin(3x) - \\cos(3x)\\bigr)$. The complementary solution is $\\kyc = C_1 \\cos(3x) + C_2 \\sin(3x)$, which duplicates the right-hand side, so try $\\kyp = x\\bigl(A\\cos(3x) + B\\sin(3x)\\bigr)$. The terms with a factor $x$ cancel when we plug in, and $\\kypdprime + 9\\kyp = -6A\\sin(3x) + 6B\\cos(3x)$. So $6B = 1$ and $-6A = 1$: $A = -\\frac{1}{6}$, $B = \\frac{1}{6}$, and $\\kyp = \\frac{x}{6}\\bigl(\\sin(3x) - \\cos(3x)\\bigr)$. The general solution is $\\ky = C_1 \\cos(3x) + C_2 \\sin(3x) + \\frac{x}{6}\\bigl(\\sin(3x) - \\cos(3x)\\bigr)$. Now $\\ky(0) = C_1 = 2$, and $\\kyprime = -3C_1 \\sin(3x) + 3C_2 \\cos(3x) + \\frac{1}{6}\\bigl(\\sin(3x) - \\cos(3x)\\bigr) + \\frac{x}{2}\\bigl(\\cos(3x) + \\sin(3x)\\bigr)$, so $\\kyprime(0) = 3C_2 - \\frac{1}{6} = 1$ and $C_2 = \\frac{7}{18}$."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "e5",
   "source_id": "2.5.5",
   "source_number": "2.5.5",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Understand",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "duplication-twice",
   "prompt": "<p>Set up the form of the particular solution but do not solve for the coefficients for $\\kyfourth-2\\kytprime+\\kydprime = e^x$.</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "solution": "$\\kyp = Ax^2 e^x$. The characteristic equation $r^4 - 2r^3 + r^2 = r^2 (r-1)^2 = 0$ has the double roots $0$ and $1$, so $\\kyc = C_1 + C_2 x + C_3 e^x + C_4 x e^x$. The guess $Ae^x$ duplicates $e^x$ and $Axe^x$ duplicates $xe^x$, so multiply by $x$ until the duplication is gone: $\\kyp = Ax^2 e^x$. (Plugging in gives $2Ae^x$, so $A = \\frac{1}{2}$, though only the form is asked for.)"
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "e6",
   "source_id": "2.5.6",
   "source_number": "2.5.6",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Understand",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "duplication-twice",
   "prompt": "<p>Set up the form of the particular solution but do not solve for the coefficients for $\\kyfourth-2\\kytprime+\\kydprime = e^x + x + \\sin x$.</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "solution": "$\\kyp = Ax^2 e^x + Bx^3 + Cx^2 + D\\cos x + E\\sin x$. As in Exercise 2.5.5, $\\kyc = C_1 + C_2 x + C_3 e^x + C_4 x e^x$, and we treat the three terms separately. For $e^x$ the guess is $Ax^2 e^x$ as before. For $x$, a general polynomial of degree 1 is $Bx + C$, but both $x$ and the constant duplicate $\\kyc$; multiplying by $x$ once leaves $Bx^2 + Cx$, whose $Cx$ still duplicates, so multiply by $x$ twice: $Bx^3 + Cx^2$. For $\\sin x$ the roots are not $\\pm i$, so $D\\cos x + E\\sin x$ has no duplication. Adding the three pieces gives the form."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "e7",
   "source_id": "2.5.7",
   "source_number": "2.5.7",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Analyze",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "vp-summary",
   "prompt": "<ol type=\"a\"><li>Using variation of parameters, find a particular solution of $\\kydprime-2\\kyprime+\\ky = e^x$.</li><li>Find a particular solution using undetermined coefficients.</li><li>Are the two solutions you found the same? See also Exercise 2.5.10.</li></ol>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "solution": "a) $\\kyp = \\frac{1}{2}x^2 e^x$. The complementary solution of $\\kydprime - 2\\kyprime + \\ky = 0$ is $\\kyc = C_1 e^x + C_2 x e^x$, so $\\kyone = e^x$, $\\kytwo = xe^x$ and $\\kf(x) = e^x$. The boxed equations are $\\kuoneprime e^x + \\kutwoprime xe^x = 0$ and $\\kuoneprime e^x + \\kutwoprime (e^x + xe^x) = e^x$. Subtracting the first from the second gives $\\kutwoprime e^x = e^x$, so $\\kutwoprime = 1$ and $\\kutwo = x$; then $\\kuoneprime = -x\\kutwoprime = -x$ and $\\kuone = -\\frac{x^2}{2}$. So $\\kyp = \\kuone \\kyone + \\kutwo \\kytwo = -\\frac{x^2}{2}e^x + x \\cdot xe^x = \\frac{1}{2}x^2 e^x$. b) Both $Ae^x$ and $Axe^x$ duplicate $\\kyc$, so try $\\kyp = Ax^2 e^x$: $\\kypprime = (2Ax + Ax^2)\\,e^x$, $\\kypdprime = (2A + 4Ax + Ax^2)\\,e^x$, and $\\kypdprime - 2\\kypprime + \\kyp = 2Ae^x$. So $A = \\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\kyp = \\frac{1}{2}x^2 e^x$. c) Yes, the two particular solutions are the same."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "e8",
   "source_id": "2.5.8",
   "source_number": "2.5.8",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Apply",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "vp-summary",
   "prompt": "<p>Find a particular solution of $\\kydprime-2\\kyprime +\\ky = \\sin (x^2)$. It is OK to leave the answer as a definite integral.</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "solution": "$\\kyp = \\int_0^x (x - t)\\,e^{x-t} \\sin(t^2)\\,dt$. With $\\kyone = e^x$, $\\kytwo = xe^x$ and $\\kf(x) = \\sin(x^2)$, the boxed equations $\\kuoneprime e^x + \\kutwoprime xe^x = 0$ and $\\kuoneprime e^x + \\kutwoprime (e^x + xe^x) = \\sin(x^2)$ give $\\kutwoprime = e^{-x}\\sin(x^2)$ and $\\kuoneprime = -x\\kutwoprime = -xe^{-x}\\sin(x^2)$. These have no closed-form antiderivatives, so write them as definite integrals: $\\kuone = -\\int_0^x t e^{-t} \\sin(t^2)\\,dt$ and $\\kutwo = \\int_0^x e^{-t} \\sin(t^2)\\,dt$. Then $\\kyp = \\kuone e^x + \\kutwo xe^x = e^x \\int_0^x (x - t)\\,e^{-t} \\sin(t^2)\\,dt = \\int_0^x (x - t)\\,e^{x-t} \\sin(t^2)\\,dt$."
   }
  },
