   "cite": "duplication-trick",
   "prompt": "<p>For an arbitrary constant $c$ find a particular solution to $\\kydprime-\\ky=e^{cx}$. Hint: Make sure to handle every possible real $c$.</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "solution": "For $c \\neq \\pm 1$, $\\kyp = \\frac{e^{cx}}{c^2 - 1}$; for $c = 1$, $\\kyp = \\frac{1}{2}xe^x$; for $c = -1$, $\\kyp = -\\frac{1}{2}xe^{-x}$. The complementary solution is $\\kyc = C_1 e^x + C_2 e^{-x}$. If $c \\neq \\pm 1$ there is no duplication: with $\\kyp = Ae^{cx}$, $\\kypdprime - \\kyp = (c^2 - 1)\\,Ae^{cx}$, so $A = \\frac{1}{c^2-1}$. If $c = 1$, the guess $Ae^x$ duplicates $\\kyc$, so try $\\kyp = Axe^x$: $\\kypdprime - \\kyp = 2Ae^x$, so $A = \\frac{1}{2}$. If $c = -1$, try $\\kyp = Axe^{-x}$: $\\kypdprime - \\kyp = -2Ae^{-x}$, so $A = -\\frac{1}{2}$."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "e10",
   "source_id": "2.5.10",
   "source_number": "2.5.10",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Analyze",
