   "cite": "moral",
   "prompt": "<ol type=\"a\"><li>Using variation of parameters, find a particular solution of $\\kydprime-\\ky = e^x$.</li><li>Find a particular solution using undetermined coefficients.</li><li>Are the two solutions you found the same? What is going on?</li></ol>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "solution": "a) $\\kyp = \\frac{1}{2}xe^x - \\frac{1}{4}e^x$. With $\\kyone = e^x$, $\\kytwo = e^{-x}$ and $\\kf(x) = e^x$, the boxed equations $\\kuoneprime e^x + \\kutwoprime e^{-x} = 0$ and $\\kuoneprime e^x - \\kutwoprime e^{-x} = e^x$ add to $2\\kuoneprime e^x = e^x$, so $\\kuoneprime = \\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\kuone = \\frac{x}{2}$; then $\\kutwoprime = -\\kuoneprime e^{2x} = -\\frac{1}{2}e^{2x}$ and $\\kutwo = -\\frac{1}{4}e^{2x}$. So $\\kyp = \\frac{x}{2}e^x - \\frac{1}{4}e^{2x} e^{-x} = \\frac{1}{2}xe^x - \\frac{1}{4}e^x$. b) The guess $Ae^x$ duplicates $\\kyc = C_1 e^x + C_2 e^{-x}$, so try $\\kyp = Axe^x$: $\\kypdprime - \\kyp = 2Ae^x$, so $A = \\frac{1}{2}$ and $\\kyp = \\frac{1}{2}xe^x$. c) No: they differ by $\\frac{1}{4}e^x$. That is fine, because $\\frac{1}{4}e^x$ solves the associated homogeneous equation $\\kydprime - \\ky = 0$. Any two particular solutions differ by a solution of the homogeneous equation, as Theorem 2.5.1 says, and both give the same general solution $\\ky = C_1 e^x + C_2 e^{-x} + \\frac{1}{2}xe^x$: the extra $-\\frac{1}{4}e^x$ is absorbed into $C_1 e^x$."
   }
  },
